[Music]  hello my name is  david bieber and i investigate  collective behavior and collective  intelligence  here at humboldt university yeah at the  moment we're at the  campus north in mitte at humboldt  university  we're here in the facilities of the  department of biology and ecology of  fish  here we investigate with the robofish  collective behavior of  fish swarms and we try to get  the rules and the mechanisms how swarms  how groups  really function with that robofish  and then we hope to apply our knowledge  also to other animals or even to humans  or to autonomously driving cars and  drones  we see here our so called robofish  which is an artificial build fish  that is aimed to interact with light  fish  and uh basically this robot consists of  two units the first unit is a fish-like  uh fish dummy a replica of a of a live  fish that we made by  3d printing and then we color it we  attach little glass eyes  on that dummy fish  and because we know or we found out that  these glass eyes really attract the live  fish  so they really accept this kind of  artificial fish as a con specific  and the second part is below this  aquarium  it's a robot unit that is basically yeah  a small card that we can steer with  a wireless lan and so uh  both parts are coupled with magnets so  at the food of this little fish there  are two magnets and on top of the robot  unit they are also a magnet  so we can steer and drag  this fish dummy through the tank and so  really control it as we want  and by having several cameras filming  the whole setup  we can give the robot the coordinates of  the live fish and then let the robot  interact with the live fish in real time  so it's autonomously  driving or swimming through the tank and  so we can test  how live fish react to different  robots that yeah behave in different  ways  yeah the first prototypes of this kind  of uh  yeah robofish they were produced in the  uk  my my boss had an  first idea on that and they tried it  with a different species with  the stickleback and  they can't use this automatic or  integrated behavior but it was  the first time they really started to  use these kind of dummy fish  that were uh driven by a robotic air  unit  to interact with live fish and then yeah  here in berlin  my boss and i we we started in  collaborating with  uh robotic engineers from the pre  university to really build  a even smaller model of the robofish  that is able to  freely interact with guppies with  mollies with other tropical fish that  are more easy to keep  in a laboratory and so step by step we  uh yeah  added things we refined the appearance  of the dummy  and yeah for example we we first started  by  painting the eyes on the dummy and the  fish didn't react  very well to that so they didn't accept  this kind of a dummy fish as a corn  specific  and then the idea was to to use a teddy  bear glass eyes  to put on the dummy and that worked so  we can put this  dummy fish inside a normal aquarium and  some of the males will start courting  this dummy so they really believe it's a  con specific  yeah it is for to to investigate  collective behavior and  collective movement behavior normally we  we can just observe huge groups or  aggregates of  animals like birds or fish or some  uh yeah zebra herds but um  we can't really manipulate the rules how  these individuals interact  we have some ideas how these rules are  but we can't really test because to test  the rules  we have to manipulate what uh the  animals really do  inside these swarms these groups  and the robot we can integrate an  individual inside these groups these  swarms  and then manipulate the rules how the  robot interacts with its neighbors  and so we can test which rules are  really important which rules  are not so important and which rules we  may have missed in our  theoretical considerations about how a  swarm is  functioning  [Music]  i mean the idea to use artificial  animals is old so even in the beginning  of  this field the ethology looking at a  behavior of animals people use some  artifacts or dummies to  stimulate live animals and with  the even better uh robotic techniques at  the moment  we saw that this is a perfect um yeah  field where we can go in and  use these new kinds of electronics  and techniques to to really bring that  field forward  and integrate such a yeah fish-like  robot into a live swarm  [Music]  whenever humans interact non-verbally  so whenever people meet and don't talk  to each other  they use similar rules that we can find  in these fish swarms we can find in uh  yeah  flocking birds or in uh other mammals in  big uh  swarms the same very same rules and they  are based on the distances  among the individuals so one of these  rules is to  um yeah go wherever you see a neighbor  individual  and when this neighboring individual is  within an optimal zone around you  you align with it then you move into the  same direction  and when it's coming too close you avoid  this individual by moving away from it  and these three simple rules by  attracting  so swimming towards or moving towards  the con specific neighbor  then aligning with it when it's in the  optimal distance and  avoiding it so moving away from it when  it's coming too close these  three simple rules they also work with  humans that was tested  also with people from our lab renting a  big  gym and inviting many people to  participate  and they were given these rules and uh  yeah they really acted as a swarm and  then we can also uh yeah  introduce leaders into these uh kind of  uh swarms  by giving some in the woodworks  information where to move  and then when all the others follow  these rules so  align with the neighbors that are around  me  and follow them then a leader that moves  to a certain  direction will keep all the others  following him  i mean we are in some situations that  simple  not in all situations but in many  situations for example when you go to a  stadium or do 